= Conclusions and Recommendations

Hallowell County's remaining broadband funds are blocked by a mapping rule, not by a lack of
money. Every township contains at least one block flagged as served, so the strict
township-wide rule makes all six ineligible; additional budget only increases the size of an
allocation that cannot legally be spent. The county's first action should therefore be
administrative: fund the small address audit, document the northern discrepancy and ask the
funding authority to adopt block-level eligibility. The audit is inexpensive relative to the
budget (#value("audit_cost"), or #value("audit_share") of it), and even a fully successful
satellite-only challenge would leave four North Ridge blocks flagged, so the rule change is a
prerequisite for construction, not an alternative to it. The audit's payoff appears only
after the rule changes: it then moves 1,464 additional households in North Ridge into the
recommended plan, which is the value of the insurance it buys.

Under block-level eligibility and the 25\% township floor, the county can add
#value("t2_main_hh") qualifying household opportunities at #value("t2_main_cost"), and the
minimum township access rate rises to 28.10\%. A 50\% floor is only feasible if verified county
evidence can override provider claims; with that overlay the plan reaches 5,750 opportunities
under the partnership.

Among the three procurement mechanisms, the county-owned backhaul partnership performs best
under the same budget, period, standard and equity constraints: #value("ppp_hh") opportunities
at \$2,306 per household, #value("advantage") more than a sole cooperative award and
#value("tender_gap") more than a lowest-cost tender. The cooperative remains a credible
fallback if lease commitments or route costs undermine the public-backbone case, and it can
compete for last-mile work under the partnership. Residents gain the most connections; the
cooperative trades a guaranteed award for an open market; the cable provider competes on equal
terms instead of collecting a construction grant; and the county accepts hub ownership,
maintenance residual and vacancy risk in exchange for a reusable asset and competition at
renewal.

Three conditions should precede construction. First, verify fixed-wireless serviceability,
because it is the parameter that most affects coverage (661 households over a ±10
percentage-point range). Second, confirm demand with the orderability, performance and
effective-use measurements rather than with subscription counts. Third, stage the awards so
that the budget, lease terms and build costs are tested before the full amount is committed.
The same evidence that makes the plan defensible also makes it revisable, which is the main
practical advantage of a transparent model over a map-based allocation.
